# quiz-sift's quality-gate sandbox.
#
# A project-owned copy of the packaged `python` Containerfile, extended with the tool set
# NFR-5 requires. It lives here rather than in .rein/oci/python/ because that directory is a
# materialized template tracked by rein.lock: editing it puts the tree in drift and `rein oci
# build --profile python` builds the packaged original anyway, silently producing an image that
# does not contain the edit. The profile points at this path with `dockerfile:` instead.
#
# The quality-gate sandbox: where repository-derived code and tests are allowed to run.
#
# Built locally with `rein oci build --profile python` — the argument names this directory,
# not the profile that uses it (the shipped `quality` profile points here via `containerfile:`).
# The command prints a digest and you paste it into the profile; it does not edit config.yaml.
# The digest — never a tag — is the binding fact: a tag would let the environment a review ran
# in change after that review was approved.
#
# What this image deliberately does NOT have: a shell-accessible host mount, network egress
# (the runner sets `--network none`), or any credential. A quality gate that needs the internet
# is not hermetic, and a test that can read your cloud token is not a test.

# Pinned by digest, not by tag. `python:3.13-slim-bookworm` is a moving target: two builds a month
# apart produced different images, so the digest pinned into config.yaml recorded whatever happened
# to be current rather than reproducing anything. This is the multi-arch index digest, so arm64 and
# amd64 hosts still each get their own layer set.
#
# Still not bit-reproducible: the apt packages below resolve against the live Debian archive, so a
# rebuild months later can pick up newer point releases. What the pin buys is that the interpreter,
# its libraries and the base filesystem cannot move underneath a recorded review — and `rein oci
# verify` catches the rest by comparing the built digest against the one the profile names.
FROM docker.io/library/python:3.13-slim-bookworm@sha256:9d7f287598e1a5a978c015ee176d8216435aaf335ed69ac3c38dd1bbb10e8d64

# A non-root user, and no way to become root: the runner also passes --security-opt
# no-new-privileges, but defence in depth is cheap here.
RUN groupadd --gid 1000 runner \
 && useradd --uid 1000 --gid 1000 --create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin runner

# uv, so a quality-gate step can install its own pinned dependency closure offline from a lock.
#
# Pinned by digest like the base image above, and for a sharper reason: a *stale* uv does not
# fail, it degrades silently. 0.9.7 could not parse a relative `exclude-newer` (uv's own
# documented supply-chain cooldown idiom), so it printed a warning and dropped the entire
# `[tool.uv]` table — then re-resolved the dependency set from scratch, ignoring the lockfile's
# timestamp cutoff. Inside the one place whose entire purpose is "the pinned environment is what
# the evidence is about", that is a divergence from the host nobody would notice: the warning is
# buried in a build log, not a failure. It is not specific to `exclude-newer` either — any key in
# that table an old parser chokes on goes the same way.
#
# Not the newest release on purpose: this repository's own `[tool.uv] exclude-newer` sets a
# one-week cooldown against just-published versions, and a pin that ignores that policy while
# shipping the image that enforces it would be saying two different things. Revisit at
# `rein upgrade` — the pin cannot notice on its own that it has aged.
COPY --from=ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv:0.11.28@sha256:0f36cb9361a3346885ca3677e3767016687b5a170c1a6b88465ec14aefec90aa /uv /usr/local/bin/uv

# The project's own conftest.py, pytest plugins, and PYTHONPATH must never reach the gate:
# an implementer who can edit a fixture the run loads can make any check pass (E2E-12).
ENV PYTEST_DISABLE_PLUGIN_AUTOLOAD=1 \
    PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 \
    PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1 \
    UV_NO_CACHE=1 \
    HOME=/home/runner

# The quality gate's tool set, baked in because the runner passes `--network none`: a step that
# would install its own linter at run time cannot run here at all. Pinned exactly, for the same
# reason the base image is — a review recorded against "whatever ruff was current" is a review of
# an environment nobody can reconstruct. These mirror the dev group of the project's
# pyproject.toml; when one moves there, this image is rebuilt and config.yaml's digest repinned,
# which is a change to the frozen environment and so happens before a mandate is approved.
#
# `pre-commit` is deliberately absent. Its hooks are cloned from GitHub on first run, so
# `pre-commit run --all-files` cannot work without egress; it belongs on the host at commit time
# and in CI. The gate runs ruff and mypy directly instead — the same check without the fetch.
RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --root-user-action=ignore \
      "pytest==9.0.3" \
      "ruff==0.15.16" \
      "mypy==2.1.0" \
      "mkdocs==1.6.1" \
      "mkdocs-material==9.7.6" \
      "mkdocstrings[python]==1.0.4"

# A non-root default for anyone who runs this image by hand. `rein` does not use it: every
# launch passes `--user <the host user's uid:gid>`, because the worktree and the 0600 control
# socket it mounts in are the host user's and no other uid can reach them.
USER 1000:1000
WORKDIR /work
ENTRYPOINT []
CMD ["python", "-c", "print('rein quality-gate sandbox')"]
